TestCase inside the set does not stop a PASS. It is named below with its proof.
Run labelled CLEAN: the bench was probe-MATCH before and after, the test links were isolated once
before the TestSet and restored once after it, and nothing was changed between TestCases.

- Copper sweep (TestCase_1–7) on stack member 1 port1.0.9 (AT-SPTXc copper SFP) <-> x230-10GP
  port1.0.4 (fixed RJ45). Baseline was a-full/a-1000. `speed 10` REJECTED
  (`% port1.0.9: Unsupported speed/duplex combination port1.0.9`). 100 and 1000 were ACCEPTED and
  linked fixed (`full 100`, `full 1000`, no a- prefix). 2500/5000/10000 were REJECTED at both ends,
  and the link stayed at a-1000.
- Fibre sweep (TestCase_8–12), measurable for the first time, on stack member 4 port4.0.26
  (AT-SPSX 1000BASE-SX, S/N A03240R192702000) <-> IE520-sa port1.0.26 (1000BASE-SX), access
  VLAN 4000. `speed 100` REJECTED by the DUT (the partner rejected it too). `speed 1000` ACCEPTED
  and linked `connected 4000 full 1000 1000BASE-SX`. 10000/40000/100000 REJECTED at both ends
  (`% port4.0.26: Unsupported speed/duplex combination port4.0.26`); the configured speed stayed
  auto, current speed 1000, link UP. The TOPOLOGY GAP was restated at dispatch: the only fibre link
  is 1G SX, so every 10G+ step can only give a rejected-speed result. That is the expected result,
  not a fibre defect.
- Fixed speed S = 1000 and the remaining behaviour (TestCase_13–33) all PASS on the copper test
  port:
  - fixed-vs-auto reporting;
  - invalid arguments (`speed fast` → `% Invalid input detected at '^' marker.`; `speed 5` and
    `speed 200000` rejected);
  - a documented-but-rejected value (10000) leaves the config and link unchanged;
  - partner mismatch at 100/100 built self-contained;
  - `shutdown`/`no shutdown` keeps `speed 1000`;
  - `no speed` and `speed auto` both return to a-full/a-1000 = the step-2 baseline;
  - per-port isolation: the monitored ports port1.0.2 and port4.0.26 are unchanged, with no
    link event in `show log`;
  - S2 = 100 relinks fixed;
  - `copy running-config startup-config`;
  - a whole-stack `reload`: port1.0.9 comes back `connected 3999 full 1000` with speed
    1000/duplex full retained, and nothing typed after boot.
- UNSUPPORTED, TestCase_20 (step 21, "incompatible fixed pair"). The case needs the DUT test port to
  accept both `speed 10` and `speed 100`. The DUT's own step-3 answer is
  `% port1.0.9: Unsupported speed/duplex combination port1.0.9` for `speed 10`, so the 10-vs-100
